---
.title = "Transports",
.description = "The netlayers that move a session's bytes between two peers, what each one authenticates and encrypts, and which of them are safe to deploy.",
.date = @date("2026-06-12T00:00:00"),
.author = "Jim Isaacs",
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

A transport, or *netlayer*, moves session bytes between two peers. The session
above it does not change. In the table, the **designator** is the peer name
inside an OCapN locator.

| Symbol | Designator | Channel | What proves the peer | Safe to deploy |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `tcp` | `host:port` | cleartext | Nothing does. | No. Keep it for testing and Goblins interoperability. |
| `keyed-tcp` | Ed25519 key, address in the `direct-addresses` hint | cleartext | Nothing; the key is only a dial target. | Only on a network you already trust. |
| `keyed-relay` | Ed25519 key, no address at all | cleartext WebSocket to the relay | Nothing; the key is only a dial target. | Only with a relay and a network you already trust. |
| `iroh` | Ed25519 node id | encrypted QUIC | The QUIC handshake itself. | Yes. |
| `webrtc` | Ed25519 key, reached through signaling | encrypted DataChannel over DTLS | The signaling authority binds the identity to the channel. | Yes, with a signaling authority you trust. |
| `inproc` | a name inside one process | in-memory pipes | Nothing, and there is nothing to prove. | No. Keep it for tests and demos. |

Two different facts sit behind that fourth column. Every pancake connection
signs its handshake with a key it draws fresh for that connection, so the
signature proves the session held together and nothing more. Naming *who* the
peer is requires an authenticated end-to-end channel. iroh reads the identity
its QUIC handshake authenticated, and WebRTC uses the identity its signaling
authority assigned. A key in a cleartext locator proves nothing by itself.

Plain `tcp` is the one to be careful with. The OCapN draft specification
requires a netlayer to keep a session secure enough that only its participants
can insert messages, and cleartext TCP does not. Upstream
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) ships no TCP netlayer,
and the OCapN test suite's equivalent is named `tcp-testing-only` and says in
its own source that it is not safe and should not be used in production. Treat
pancake's `tcp` the same way: it exists so live checks can run against outside
peers.

`keyed-tcp` and `keyed-relay` do not authenticate the peer or encrypt the
bytes. Anyone on the path can read every frame and change it. On `keyed-relay` that
includes the relay operator, who sees plaintext even when the browser leg is
`wss://`. Use iroh when a native channel must be confidential, and WebRTC for a
direct browser channel. A browser can also reach a native peer through the
byte-transparent [relay]($link.page('relay')); ADR-0107 proposes Noise_XK to
seal that byte channel end to end.

[Choosing a transport]($link.page('transports/choosing')) turns the table into
a decision. The [iroh]($link.page('transports/iroh')) and
[WebRTC]($link.page('transports/webrtc')) pages cover setup and limits.
[Modules]($link.page('modules')) explains how netlayers plug into the driver.
